  "worked_solution": "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24=-3(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the two critical points $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-5,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-5) & =-3(-5+4)(-5-2)=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =-3(0+4)(0-2)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =-3(3+4)(3-2)=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2198$ | min | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n* At $x=-4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-4$ is a local minimum.\n\n* At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
